dx - Run Bazel workflows

dx <command> [flags] [scope ...] [-- bazel-options ...]

Scopes: labels, patterns, files, or dirs. No scope means //... for most commands.

Exit codes: 0 success, 2 usage error, 1 failed check.

Usage: dx [COMMAND]

Commands:
  security    run security audit over resolved scopes (non-mutating; live Gitleaks plus advisory/vuln backends)
  license     run license audit over resolved scopes (non-mutating; live license-policy plus SPDX backend)
  lint        run lint analysis over resolved scopes (check by default; --apply writes fixes)
  typecheck   run typecheck analysis over resolved scopes (check by default; --apply writes fixes)
  format      check or rewrite formatting over resolved scopes (check by default; --apply writes fixes)
  generate    update BUILD declarations from source with Gazelle (check by default; --apply writes)
  build       run Bazel build over resolved targets
  test        run Bazel test over resolved targets
  coverage    collect LCOV coverage with optional threshold
  run         build and run runnable targets sequentially (explicit labels/patterns; file/dir scopes need exactly one runnable)
  deploy      build and run a single deployable target
  check       run generate+format+lint+typecheck checks in order (non-mutating)
  fix         apply generate+format+lint+typecheck fixes in order (check by default; --apply writes fixes, then verifies with a read-only check)
  clean       prune unselected managed state, never Bazel outputs; use `bazel clean` for those (check by default; --apply prunes; no scopes)
  update      update dependencies per set through qualified resolvers (check by default; --apply writes updates)
  bump        widen one declared requirement to a new version (check by default; --apply widens)
  add         declare one new requirement at an exact version (check by default; --apply adds)
  migrate     rewrite breaking changes across releases (upgrade-only; check by default; --apply migrates)
  codegen     materialize declared generated source (check by default; --apply writes)
  env         collect the managed development environment (check by default; --apply writes managed state)
  init        adopt dx into an existing tree (absent-only; check by default; --apply scaffolds)
  new         start a new project for one language (absent-only; check by default; --apply scaffolds)
  upgrade     one-shot pin+migrate+env+codegen composition with recovery pointer (check by default; --apply upgrades)
  status      report workspace status; --verbose adds command capabilities; without .dx/version it exits 1 with status_pin_mismatch
  version     report version and pin drift
  watch       watch for changes and rebuild (local only)
  completion  emit shell completions from the CLI grammar (bash|zsh|fish|powershell)
  docs        build, check, and serve the mdBook documentation site (non-mutating; --check validates without rendering)
  list-tests  list configured test targets without building or running them (non-mutating; --cases builds and runs supported test binaries to list cases)
  rollback    restore the previous recorded pin (check by default; --apply restores)
  worktree    lease warm checkout slots (add, release, list, path, prune; prune checks by default, --apply deletes)

Options:
  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Environment:
  DX_WORKSPACE=<dir>
      Default for --workspace.
  DX_QUIET=<bool>
      Default for --quiet.
  DX_VERBOSE=<bool>
      Default for --verbose.
  DX_COLOR=<mode>
      Default for --color.
  DX_OUTPUT=<mode>
      Default for --output.
  DX_FAIL_ON=<level>
      Default for --fail-on.
  <bool> is on for true and off for false.
      Anything else is a usage error. An empty value is unset.
  NO_COLOR=<any>
      Disable color output.
  BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY=<dir>
      Workspace start under `bazel run`.
  BUILD_WORKING_DIRECTORY=<dir>
      Current directory under `bazel run`.
  dx.toml (committed) and dx.local.toml (local-only, gitignored) carry the same defaults as the DX_ variables, below the environment.
      Local beats committed. In CI the local file and DX_ variables are ignored; flags still win.
      An unknown key is a usage error.
